  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: PatPre4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "When talking to your oncologist about clinical trials, the topic of
    animal studies (in vivo) comes up. She explains that these studies look at how
    a treatment works inside a whole, living body rather than just in a lab dish
    of cells \u2014 before it is tested in people.\r\n\r\n<b>Why is it important
    for researchers to test new treatments in animals (in vivo) before human trials?</b>\r\n"
  explanation: Animal studies help protect patients by showing how a new treatment
    works inside a living body before it is ever tested in people. They allow researchers
    to look for possible side effects, learn what dose might be safest, and understand
    how different parts of the body respond. This information helps scientists design
    safer clinical trials and improves the chances that new treatments will be both
    helpful and safe for patients.
  correctAnswer: They help researchers understand how a treatment might affect organs,
    systems, and overall health in a living body before human testing
  incorrectAnswer1: They show researchers if a new treatment will work in patients
  incorrectAnswer2: They allow researchers to skip lab testing and move forward into
    clinical trials faster
  incorrectAnswer3: They show researchers how diverse patient populations will respond
    to a new treatment
  questionRole: Patient
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: PreClinical
